Scope of the port
-----------------

Ported 1:1 from upstream:

- `TypeSafeClient` -- request construction, response parsing, and the
  status-code-to-exception-type mapping
- `TypeSafeConfig` -- configuration fields and construction-time validation
- `Types` -- request and response data structures (including `ModelCard`)
- `RetryPolicy` and the retry/backoff logic
- `TypeSafeException` and its subtypes
- Log level filtering and redaction of sensitive headers (`authorization`,
  `proxy-authorization`, `x-api-key`)

Added on the Kotlin side, with no upstream counterpart:

- `ProxySpec` -- HTTP and SOCKS5 proxying. Ktor's `ProxyConfig` carries no
  credentials (on the JVM it is `java.net.Proxy`), so proxy authentication drops
  down to OkHttp's `proxyAuthenticator` to answer the 407 challenge
- A JVM target alongside Android, so the library is usable from a server as well
  as from an app. `defaultHttpClient` consequently lives in `commonMain` with no
  `expect`/`actual` split: OkHttp serves both targets, and Kotlin does not permit
  a source set shared between JVM and Android targets in any case
- Kotlin naming for fields such as `ModelCard.releaseDate`, which maps 1:1 onto
  the upstream `release_date`

Behaviours **deliberately preserved** from upstream: every failure path has the
same semantics, including the status-code-to-exception mapping table. Changing
those would make error handling diverge between the two SDKs, which defeats the
purpose of a port.


Dependency footprint
--------------------

This library does not depend on any application code. Its only dependencies are:

    kotlinx-serialization-json
    kotlinx-coroutines-core
    ktor-client-core
    ktor-client-okhttp

The default `baseUrl` (`https://api.typesafe.ai`) comes from upstream and is the
official endpoint for that service.


